{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffxnGFTV8mE5XjnAe8YJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passes the admission self-check (`OK`). On its short test fit it scored ELPD-LOO −3418.0, against the incumbent's −3435.5 from a full fit. That isn't a fair comparison yet: the smoke fit was 100 draws on one chain, and it flagged its own LOO estimate as unreliable, with 12% of trials above the Pareto-k tolerance. Convergence, novelty against the live models and fit speed aren't covered by the check, so we'll only know those when admission runs the full fit. The check took about 9 minutes on the single CPU available here.\n\n**Model: `tally_span_switch_periodic_triplet_variety`** (refines the incumbent `tally_span_switch_ideal_periodic_unit`)\n\n- **Kept unchanged from the incumbent:**\n  - each person's expected span for the running heads-minus-tails tally, with length-scaled sensitivity;\n  - a shared ideal switching rate with a person-specific weight;\n  - the shared penalty on sequences that repeat a short unit;\n  - each person's left/right lean.\n- **The one change:** a shared reward on the share of distinct overlapping three-flip chunks, taken from the rival it is statistically tied with, `tally_span_switch_ideal_triplet_variety`. This turns the incumbent's all-or-none \"is it periodic?\" judgement into a graded one: sequences that are nearly patterned, or reuse the same few chunks, now also look less random.\n- **Why this change:** I tried several extra cues in a quick pooled fit (no person-specific effects). Periodicity plus triplet variety was the best pair: about 43 nats better than periodicity alone and about 10 better than triplet variety alone. It also goes after the critique's one flagged discrepancy, that responses to individual pairs are more polarised than the incumbent predicts. That discrepancy is weak, though (p = 0.05, and it doesn't survive the FDR correction, q = 0.29).